            <ArticleTitle>Delay in an Eating Disorder Diagnosis: The Reason Was a &quot;Shox&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-3188(17)30507-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2017.11.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Patients' underlying medical conditions might affect the presentation and progression of an eating disorder.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We describe a patient with an undiagnosed, rare, genetic skeletal dysplasia with effects on body mass index that likely led to body image distortion and delayed the diagnosis of an eating disorder.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is critical to fully assess disordered eating in the context of each patient's clinical status.</AbstractText>
